const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',      url: '/' },
  { name: 'Questions', url: '/questions/' },
  { name: 'Are Ravens Nocturnal?', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Do ravens come out at night?', a: 'Ravens are diurnal (day-active) birds and are not normally active after dark. They roost at communal roosting sites from dusk to dawn. However, ravens do move conspicuously at dawn and dusk — flying to and from roost sites — which may give the impression of night activity. In urban areas with strong artificial lighting, ravens may occasionally forage at dusk or early evening, but this is not typical behaviour. At summer latitudes with very long days (Iceland, Alaska), ravens can be active at "night" because there is no actual darkness.' },
  { q: 'Where do ravens sleep at night?', a: 'Ravens sleep at roosting sites — often elevated perches in trees, on cliff ledges, or on rocky outcrops. Non-breeding ravens use communal roosts where many birds gather. Breeding pairs may roost separately from large groups, roosting on or near the nest during incubation and brooding, or at an adjacent perch. Communal roost sites are often used repeatedly over many seasons, suggesting site fidelity for roosts as well as nest sites.' },
  { q: 'Are crows or ravens active at night?', a: 'Neither. Crows and ravens are both diurnal. Owls occupy the nighttime corvid-equivalent niche — Great Horned Owls in North America and Eagle Owls in Eurasia are large, intelligent, visually hunting birds active at night. Interestingly, ravens are themselves preyed upon by Great Horned Owls and Eagle Owls at their roost sites, suggesting the transition from diurnal to nocturnal activity is a vulnerability period.' },
];
