    <h1>Are Ravens Nocturnal? Raven Activity Patterns</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> No — ravens are diurnal (day-active) birds. They are most active from shortly after dawn to late afternoon. At dusk, ravens fly to communal or pair roosts where they spend the night. Visible raven flights at dawn and dusk — the commute to and from roosts — may give an impression of night activity, but actual foraging and social behaviour is confined to daylight hours.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Daily Activity Pattern</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Raven activity follows a broadly consistent diurnal pattern:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pre-dawn to dawn:</strong> birds begin moving from roost, with pre-dawn calling sometimes audible. Departure from communal roosts often occurs as a group, with many birds leaving simultaneously.</li>
      <li><strong>Morning (dawn to ~10 am):</strong> peak foraging period; corvids are most active early when thermals are building and food has not yet been displaced by competitors. Early-morning flights to known food sources or exploration flights.</li>
      <li><strong>Midday:</strong> activity reduces, particularly in warm weather. Ravens rest, preen, and socialise. In hot desert environments (Sonoran Desert) midday inactivity is marked.</li>
      <li><strong>Afternoon:</strong> second foraging peak; ravens may return to cached food or visit new sites discovered in the morning.</li>
      <li><strong>Evening (before dark):</strong> return flights to roost. Communal roosts may show significant activity — calling, displacing, finding positions — in the hour before full dark.</li>
      <li><strong>Night:</strong> roosting; inactive. Ravens are vulnerable to owl predation during nighttime roosting.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Roosting Behaviour</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens use two types of roosting arrangements:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Communal roosts:</strong> used primarily by non-breeding floater ravens and sometimes by territorial birds outside the breeding season. These roosts can aggregate dozens to hundreds of birds at a single site. The site may be used night after night for many weeks or months. Communal roosting confers benefits including predator detection (many-eyes) and information sharing about food locations.</li>
      <li><strong>Pair roosts:</strong> territorial breeding pairs typically roost near the nest during breeding season or at a consistent perch near their territory core outside it. These are quieter and less conspicuous than communal roosts.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Exceptions: Long Summer Days</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> At high northern latitudes (Iceland, northern Norway, Alaska), summer days are extremely long — with minimal or no true darkness near the summer solstice. Ravens in these regions are active throughout the light period, which can effectively mean activity for 20+ hours per day in midsummer. This is not nocturnal behaviour but an extension of diurnal activity under unusual light conditions.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

